WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.140
It takes a long time to make a movie, even
if a production does completely stay on schedule,

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.440
so if a scene proves to be more complicated
than others, things can get out of control

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.520
relatively quickly.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.040
Every now and again, you'll hear of massive
delays and setbacks that emerge mid-shoot.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.380
Whether it's due to on-set injuries or a centerpiece
action scene eating up huge amounts of extra

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.560
time, or perhaps a demanding director who
just wants several dozen takes to have some

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.450
choices in the editing room, unforeseen circumstances
surrounding a scene can make a movie's production

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.020
process go right off the rails.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.550
Here are some movie scenes that ended up being
unexpectedly massive undertakings for the

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:36.420
cast and crew.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.300
Spidey Senses

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.100
Before there was the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
Peter Parker swung onto screens in director

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.760
Sam Raimi's 2002 blockbuster Spider-Man.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.220
The film helped usher in the new superhero
movie renaissance, since Hollywood was still

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.540
trying to figure out what comic book movie
audiences really wanted to see.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.440
Similarly in flux was the state of special
effects.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.360
Though the "digital revolution" rolled out
through the '90s, CGI was still undergoing

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.900
the occasional growing pains at the turn of
the millennium.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.960
Many of Spidey's stunts would be achieved
digitally, but they don't exactly hold up

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.750
when compared to what the webhead gets up
to in his newer adventures.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.720
Raimi has often spun his fascination with
sleight-of-hand magic into low-budget camera

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.040
tricks, but he was determined to achieve Peter
Parker's "Spidey Sense" for real in the film.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:24.400
For the scene in which his newly enhanced
agility allows Peter to rescue Mary Jane from

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:29.250
a slip and catch her entire airborne lunch
neatly on a tray, no computer assistance was

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:29.750
used.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:34.260
Maguire managed the trick himself with some
adhesive and props dropped strategically from

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:34.760
above.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.440
Of course, that doesn't mean it wasn't difficult
— the shot took 156 takes over a 16-hour

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.180
shooting day.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:42.940
True abyss

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.700
The true story of the R.M.S.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.630
Titanic is often seen as a warning about mankind's
hubris.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.680
The massive vessel was built to break records
and demonstrate English superiority while

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.850
catering to the wealthiest people, and it
was woefully unprepared for the disaster that

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.360
ensued when it struck an iceberg.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:03.380
During production, James Cameron's 1997 film
Titanic seemed destined to fall victim to

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.540
ambition as well.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.720
Cameron intended to set an original love story
against the backdrop of a painstaking recreation

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.400
of the original ship and its sinking.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.400
Elaborate sets, difficult conditions, and
Cameron's exacting perfectionism saddled the

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.630
project with a constantly bloating budget
and a schedule that spiraled out of control,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.560
as it became the most expensive movie ever
made at the time.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:28.700
Surprisingly, while the intricate ship sets
and harrowing sinking scenes provided Titanic's

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:33.730
most dazzling moments, the most difficult
scenes to shoot involved no effects or re-creations

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:34.230
at all.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.040
In fact, it was Cameron himself who embarked
on a dozen expeditions to the real shipwreck,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.600
more than two miles below the ocean's surface,
over the course of two years to research material

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:44.240
for the film.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:52.190
"I was struck just by the surreal nature of
the fact that I was sitting here on this deck."

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:57.080
On these lengthy and often dangerous dives,
Cameron shot extensive footage of the Titanic's

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.360
present state for use in the movie's opening
scenes.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.260
The risks paid off, as the film became an
unprecedented hit, hailed for its grandeur

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.900
and spectacle and earning more than its due
at the box office.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.160
But it's hard to imagine anyone else besides
James Cameron who'd commit to such an arduous

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.260
undertaking just to get a few authentic shots.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.400
Spielberg sinking

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.240
Not only did Steven Spielberg's Jaws define
the modern summer blockbuster, it also demonstrated

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.020
how to create tension by withholding the reveal
of what the audience is afraid of.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.240
This wasn't entirely intentional, as the mechanical
shark's frequent breakdowns forced Spielberg

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.660
to show the beast much less than he originally
planned.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.740
The young director was forced to develop clever
ways to suggest the man-eater's presence without

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.200
the use of the troubled robot.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.160
One of these quick fixes ironically led to
the most disastrous day of an already beleaguered

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:45.660
production.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.220
The heroic fishermen harpoon the shark with
a series of floating barrels that signal its

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.180
presence beneath the surface.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.260
For a scene in which the ominous floaters
circle the boat known as the Orca, the film's

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:00.190
effects technicians affixed a pulley system
that controlled their movement to the bottom

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:00.820
of the hull.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.180
Unfortunately, the planks of the Orca weren't
strong enough to support this rig, and a quick

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.180
yank on the barrel's ropes tore a hole in
the boat's hull.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.720
It began to sink rapidly, sending the crew
scrambling to rescue the cast, equipment,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.660
and an entire day's footage from the sea.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.810
Although this and several other disasters
pushed the shooting schedule from the planed

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:25.639
55 days to a whopping final tally of 159 days,
the Orca was repaired, the footage was saved,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.660
and Jaws went on to reshape movie history.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.480
Disney's near-disaster

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.020
Fantasia, the third animated feature from
Walt Disney Studios, is a marvel on every

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.380
level, particularly in the context of its
1940 production.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.580
It may have been underappreciated at the time,
but the achievement of Fantasia's marriage

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.840
of technical innovation with artistic ambition
is hard to deny.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:50.520
For the grand finale of their "concert feature,"
Disney and his artists envisioned a climactic

00:04:50.529 --> 00:04:55.830
clash between the sacred and profane, pairing
Modest Mussorgsky's "Night on Bald Mountain"

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.920
with Franz Schubert's "Ave Maria," and it
was quite an undertaking.

00:04:59.749 --> 00:05:04.399
The visuals for "Ave Maria" called for the
longest single shot in the history of animation

00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:08.990
at the time, as the camera followed a procession
through a densely layered forest and into

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:10.280
a cathedral of trees.

00:05:11.009 --> 00:05:15.139
This meant that a line-up of background paintings
had to be set up end-to-end across the length

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.460
of a huge soundstage.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.360
The scene was completed once, only to have
the horrified crew realize they had put the

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:22.660
wrong lens on the camera.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.320
A second attempt was foiled by an earthquake.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.780
All the while, the scene was frequently halted
so that the soundstage could be used for Walt's

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.240
weekly badminton game.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.960
Needless to say, the final shot took way longer
than anyone expected, and in fact, it was

00:05:35.961 --> 00:05:40.270
only achieved in just enough time for the
film reels to be flown to New York mere hours

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.270
before Fantasia's premiere.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.500
Now, that's a close call.

